# Responsibilities of a Processor and Controller-Processor Contract Requirements

## Processor Responsibilities

According to GDPR Article 28(3), a processor must:

(a) Process personal data only on documented instructions from the controller, including regarding transfers to third countries, unless required by Union or Member State law (in which case the processor must inform the controller beforehand, unless prohibited by law on grounds of public interest) (GDPR, Article 28(3)(a));

(b) Ensure that persons authorized to process personal data are bound by confidentiality or statutory obligation of confidentiality (GDPR, Article 28(3)(b));

(c) Take all measures required pursuant to Article 32 (security measures) (GDPR, Article 28(3)(c));

(d) Respect conditions for engaging sub-processors (GDPR, Article 28(3)(d));

(e) Assist the controller with appropriate technical and organisational measures to fulfill the controller's obligation to respond to data subject rights requests (GDPR, Article 28(3)(e));

(f) Assist the controller in ensuring compliance with Articles 32 to 36, taking into account the nature of processing and information available to the processor (GDPR, Article 28(3)(f));

(g) Delete or return all personal data to the controller after services end, or delete existing copies, unless Union or Member State law requires storage (GDPR, Article 28(3)(g));

(h) Make available all information necessary to demonstrate compliance and allow for audits conducted by the controller or mandated auditor (GDPR, Article 28(3)(h)).

## Required Contract Contents

A controller-processor contract must be in writing (including electronic form) and set out (GDPR, Article 28(3)):

- The subject-matter and duration of processing
- The nature and purpose of processing
- The type of personal data and categories of data subjects
- The obligations and rights of the controller

The contract may be based on standard contractual clauses adopted by the Commission or a supervisory authority (GDPR, Article 28(6), (7), (8)).

